# 5. The evidential layer

The first four tutorials treated Turbulance as an ordinary small language:
values, operators, control flow, and the three commands that let you interrogate
a run. This one takes up the part that is not ordinary. A proposition collects
motions, verdicts contact those motions, and the run reports a score for each
one.

Every claim below was checked against the code and against a real run. Where a
surface behaves unlike the obvious guess, the guess is named rather than
smoothed over. This tutorial has more of those than any of the previous four,
and two of them will change what you write.

## `point`: a claim with a confidence attached

A point is a claim you can carry a number alongside. It requires a map literal.

```turbulance
point claim = {
    text: "the sample is contaminated",
    certainty: 0.7
}
point unmeasured = {text: "the blank was clean"}
print(claim.text, claim.confidence)
print(unmeasured.confidence, claim.nosuch)
```


```output
the sample is contaminated 0.7
1 none

claim = point(claim, conf=0.7)
unmeasured = point(unmeasured, conf=1.0)
```


Three things there are worth naming.

`certainty` is the key you write; `confidence` is the field you read. Both
field names read the same value, so `a.confidence` and `a.certainty` are the same
number, and it is the confidence the point was built with rather than whatever
the map happens to hold under that key.

The confidence is clamped to the range 0 to 1, and clamping is silent.
`certainty: 5` does not raise; it gives a confidence of 1. If you write a
percentage by mistake, nothing will tell you.

A point without a `certainty` key is confidence 1. Not zero, and not an
error. An unmeasured claim is taken at full strength unless you say otherwise.

Any other field reads out of the map, and a field that is not there gives
`none` rather than raising.

## `proposition` and `motion`

A proposition names a question. The motions inside it are the specific claims
the run can contact.

```turbulance
proposition Contamination:
    motion Peak("a peak at 214 appears in three replicates")
    motion Blank("the blank shows the same peak")
```


```output
Peak = motion(Peak)
Blank = motion(Blank)
```


### Motions are lifted out of the block

This is the first surprise, and it shows up in the store delta above: the
motions are there as items, and the proposition is not.

Parsing a proposition partitions its block. Every `motion` statement is
lifted out into a separate list; everything else stays as the body. The run then
declares the motions first, then the proposition, then executes the body. So in
the trace the motion at line 21 is declared before the proposition at line 20 --
the proposition's own event fires at its own line, after the events of the lines
below it.

The value written for the proposition is the list of its motion names as
strings. Not the block, and not a score.

### The proposition name is not bound

The second surprise, and the one most likely to catch you. `declare-proposition` writes
to the trace, not to the store. So this fails:

    resolve P

with `undefined name 'P'`. The proposition exists as a structure the run tracks; it is
not a value you can name in an expression. The motions are values; the
proposition is not.

### Motion text is optional

All three of these parse:

    motion Bare
    motion Empty()
    motion Full("a peak at 214 appears in three replicates")

The first two give a motion whose text is the empty string. A motion carries
exactly two readable fields, `name` and `text`. Any other field gives
`none` silently -- no error, no warning. `Bare.nosuch` prints
`none` and moves on.

## Verdicts: `support`, `contradict`, `inconclusive`

A verdict contacts one motion by name.

```turbulance
proposition Replicates:
    motion Consistent("the peak appears in all three")

    support Consistent with_confidence(0.5)
    support Consistent with_confidence(0.5)
```


```output
Consistent = motion(Consistent)
```


The default confidence is 1. `with_confidence` sets it, and the value is clamped
the same way a point's is.

### The score is noisy-or, not a sum

This is the surprise that will change what you write. Look at the trace:

    support         writes Replicates.Consistent = 0.5
    support         writes Replicates.Consistent = 0.5
    resolve-motion  writes Replicates.Consistent = 0.75

Two supports of 0.5 give 0.75. Not 1.0, and not 0.5. The supports are combined
as one minus the product of their complements, and the contradicts are combined
the same way and multiplied in as a complement:

    score = noisy_or(supports) * (1 - noisy_or(contradicts))

So evidence accumulates with diminishing returns and can approach 1 without
reaching it, and a single contradict of 1.0 drives the score to 0 no matter how
much support there is. Adding a third weak support to a motion that already has
two moves the score less than the first one did. That is the intended reading:
two independent halves of a case are stronger than one, but not twice as strong.

### `inconclusive` is zero, and takes no confidence

`inconclusive` parses a motion name and nothing else. There is no
`with_confidence` form for it, and it forces the confidence to 0.

That is not the same as declining to record a verdict. It records one, at zero.

### Two verdicts on one motion collapse in the reads

The third surprise, and it is visible only in the trace. The third cell above
already does it:

    support Consistent with_confidence(0.5)
    support Consistent with_confidence(0.5)

and the `resolve-motion` event reports its reads as a single entry for
`Consistent` carrying 0.5 -- the last verdict's confidence, not both. The
reads are keyed by motion name, so the second verdict overwrites the first in
that view.

The score is still computed from every verdict. It is 0.75, which is only
reachable from two of them. But if you read the `resolve-motion` reads
expecting a list of everything that contributed, you will see one entry and
conclude there was one verdict.

The consequence for reading a trace: the `support` and `contradict` events
are the record of what was said. The `resolve-motion` event is the record of
what it came to. Do not read the second for the first.

Here is the case where that matters most:

```turbulance
proposition Ordering:
    motion Blank("the blank shows the same peak")

    contradict Blank with_confidence(0.8)
    inconclusive Blank
```


```output
```


That output block is empty, and the reason is worth a paragraph of its own
below. First the verdicts.

The score for `Blank` ends at 0. The contradict of 0.8 would have given 0.2
on its own; the `inconclusive` that follows it is the last verdict, and its
zero is what the score reflects. Order matters, and the collapsed reads will
show you only the zero.

## `hypothesis`

A hypothesis is a named block. It does not partition.

```turbulance
hypothesis Instrument:
    item column_age = 400
    motion Drift("retention times drift after 300 runs")
```


```output
column_age = 400
Drift = motion(Drift)
```


Two differences from a proposition. The motion inside is not lifted -- it is
declared in place, in the order it is written. And the value written for the
hypothesis is its own name as a string, not a list and not the block.

A hypothesis is a label over a region of a script. It groups; it does not score.

## `resolve`

`resolve` takes an expression, not a name:

    resolve a.confidence * 2

It evaluates the expression and emits a `resolve` event carrying the
result as its note. It writes nothing to the store. Structurally it is a bare
expression statement under a different rule name, which is the point: it marks a
line as the place a determination was reached, so a search of the trace can find
it.

Since the proposition name is not bound, `resolve P` does not work. Resolve
the thing you actually want to report -- a point's confidence, an arithmetic
combination of several -- and let the `resolve-motion` events carry the
per-motion scores.

## A verdict outside a proposition

    support Stray with_confidence(1)

raises `support outside a proposition`. Worth knowing precisely when: the confidence expression is
evaluated first, and only then is the enclosing proposition checked. So a stray
verdict whose confidence is itself broken reports the confidence's error, not
this one. The line number in the message is the verdict's line either way.

## The store delta reports change, not content

The empty output block above is not the proposition being skipped. It is the
delta doing what it says.

A delta reports what a cell *changed*. `Blank` was already declared in an
earlier cell, and this cell declared it again with the same text, so nothing
changed and nothing is reported. Three cases:

    Fresh = motion(Fresh)                        a name that is new
    Other = motion(Other)  (was motion(Other))   a name whose value changed
                                                  an unchanged name: omitted

The middle case is nearly useless for a motion, and it is worth seeing why. A
motion renders as its name alone, so both halves read `motion(Other)` even
though the text differs. The delta tells you something changed without telling
you what. For a motion or a point, the trace is where the content is.

An empty output block therefore means the cell changed no name. It ran, it
deposited, and its verdicts are in the trace.

## What reaches the store

Points and motions are values, so they appear in the store delta below a cell,
rendered as `point(claim, conf=0.7)` and `motion(Peak)`. Propositions do not appear
at all, and neither do the per-motion scores.

The scores live in the trace, as `resolve-motion` events writing a dotted
item name:

    P.Peak = 0.75
    P.Blank = 0

So `ndombolo trace` is not an optional extra for the evidential layer the way it
was for arithmetic. It is the only place the scores are reported. The output
block below a cell will show you the motions and never the verdicts.

## Things to try

- Give a motion two supports of 0.5 and check the score is 0.75. Then add a
  third and note that it moves less than the second did.
- Add `contradict M with_confidence(1)` to a motion with heavy support and watch the score go to 0.
- Put `inconclusive` after a `contradict` on the same motion, then before
  it, and compare the `resolve-motion` reads in each case.
- Read `Bare` with a field that does not exist and confirm it prints
  `none` rather than raising.
- Write `certainty: 5` and confirm from the trace that the stored confidence is 1.
- Try `resolve P` and read the error. Then resolve a point's confidence
  instead.
- Graph a document with a proposition in it and see which of these names became
  items.

The next tutorial takes up the keywords that parse and do nothing, and why they
are in the grammar at all.
